Every file in that chain is right, and in order. One thing you left as a plain "fires the request": in admin.js, that function doesn't just fetch and read JSON like the rest of the app does.

```
const res = await fetch('/admin/orders.csv', { credentials: 'include' });
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(await res.text());
return res.blob();
```

It reads the response as a blob, raw bytes, not parsed data, because a file download isn't something you hand back as a JS object. The page then takes that blob and saves it. Given that, what do you reckon tells the browser to save the file to disk rather than just showing the raw CSV text in the tab, the way it would if you typed that URL in directly?

Now server/middleware/requireAdmin.js, the one that was not in the plan. Before I explain what it added - is the difference between authentication and authorisation clear to you?

That's the concept exactly, and it's exactly what this file is for.

```
export function requireAdmin(req, res, next) {
  if (req.user?.role !== 'admin') return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
  next();
}
```

requireAuth already ran earlier and proved who's asking, this one is the separate check of whether that person's role is admin, and without it any signed-in user, not just admins, could have hit the export route and pulled every order in the table. It wasn't something you asked for, the agent added it on its own, so there was no way your prediction could have caught it. Whether to keep it guarding this route is your call, not mine, I'm not touching any code here.

Last one. Your teammate says the CSV should not include customer emails. Which file do you edit to drop that column?
